WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
Injuries to the head, neck, or spine are among the most serious situations you'll face

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.360
because they can affect the central nervous system.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.960
These injuries can be external, with obvious bleeding or swelling,

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.720
but they are often internal and invisible, like a concussion,

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.639
which happens when the brain is rapidly shaken inside the skull.

00:00:18.639 --> 00:00:23.360
Whether the cause is a car accident, a fall, or a sports-related collision,

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.920
your assessment skills are vital here.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.040
If someone has a headache, feels dazed or confused,

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.040
or is experiencing nausea and vomiting, you should suspect a head injury.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.959
For injuries lower down, look for localized pain, bruising,

00:00:36.959 --> 00:00:40.160
or any numbness or tingling in their arms or legs,

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.040
which is a major red flag for a spine injury.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.520
For severe injuries, your priorities should be to call 911 immediately

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.919
and keep the person as still as possible.

00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:53.360
This is the most important thing you can do to prevent further damage.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.560
It's also a good idea to send someone to get an AED,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.880
just in case the situation escalates.

00:00:58.959 --> 00:01:01.040
Only move them if the scene becomes unsafe,

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.160
or if you absolutely must perform life-saving CPR.

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:07.760
There is one other exception to the don't move them rule,

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.519
and that is if the person begins to vomit and cannot clear their airway.

00:01:11.519 --> 00:01:16.320
In that specific case, you may need to roll them as a single unit onto their side.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.360
The goal is to keep the head, neck, and spine perfectly aligned during the roll,

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.800
moving them like a log rather than twisting any part of the body.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.239
Stay with them and keep them calm until EMS takes over.

